atency_ms": 1, "tokens_before": 23306, "tokens_after": 23306, "ok": true, "error": ""}], "final_claim": {"task_id": "t_2025d7ec", "decision": "absent", "mechanism": "cascade", "entities": [798, 131], "window": [4302, 42701], "confidence": 0.9, "evidence": [{"trace_id": "step_01", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_02", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_03", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_04", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_05", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_06", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_07", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_08", "role": "support"}], "narrative": "No time-respecting cascade exists from 798 to 131. Under the 600-unit relay constraint, forward temporal exploration from 798 reaches only 3 nodes and never touches 131 (step_01). Relaxing the constraint entirely still fails: with unlimited waiting 798 temporally reaches 650 nodes, yet 131 is not among them (step_02), so the failure is not a matter of gaps being slightly too long but of edge ordering/direction. Confounders were tested and rejected: (a) static connectivity exists \u2014 the 2-hop neighbourhood shows 798-389 and 389-131 edges via the very high-degree hub 389 (step_03) \u2014 but this is a time-ignoring artefact; (b) both endpoints are active and their activity bins overlap in the mid-window (steps 05, 06), so absence is not due to one node being silent; (c) directionality is decisive \u2014 131's only incoming contacts come from 972, 1877 and 1932 (step_07), none of which lie on any outgoing chain from 798, whose only out-partners are 45 and 389 (step_08). Both nodes are low-volume (4 and 7 events, step_04), so the apparent link is hub-mediated coincidence rather than influence transfer."}, "raw_final_response": "```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_2025d7ec\",\n  \"decision\": \"absent\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"cascade\",\n  \"entities\": [798, 131],\n  \"window\": [4302, 42701],\n  \"confidence\": 0.9,\n  \"evidence\": [\n  